[# Section 5 — analytics, columnar formats, and bridges.
   Authored 2026-05-22 against the v0.8.0-dev branch.
   Anchored in spec/misc/table-api.md +
   spec/core/data-bin.md §3.10-3.13 + spec/streaming.md.
   Cross-links use [[anchor]] form. #]

[section id=analytics n=5 title='Analytics, columnar formats, and bridges'

  [child id=overview n=5.1 title='The analytics lane'
    [intro
      """CX has an analytics lane. The same brackets that carry a config file
         also carry a typed columnar table; the same canonical-bytes-plus-hash
         identity that anchors a single document also anchors a billion-row
         dataset; the same evaluator that templates a Markdown report also
         streams over a multi-gigabyte file in bounded memory. The lane has
         three load-bearing pieces, and this section is about how they
         compose."""]

    [body
      """The three pieces are: a **logical data shape** (the `:table` type
         tag, with typed columns and nullable cells); a **physical wire
         format** (CXCol, the column-oriented streaming binary form,
         tag `0x63` in [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md) §3.11);
         and **bridges to the broader ecosystem** (Apache Arrow C Data
         Interface for zero-copy interop, Apache Parquet for cross-tool
         storage)."""]

    [list
      [item """**Logical**: `[orders :table :columns (...) (...) ...]` — see
              [[table-shape]]. Authored in CX text, parses into the same
              AST as any other CX element, validates against `.cxs`
              schemas, hashes via SHA-256 over canonical bytes."""]
      [item """**Physical**: CXCol — the column-oriented streaming
              binary form. Row-group chunked, optionally zstd-compressed
              per chunk, optionally schema-driven for tag-density.
              See [[cxcol]]."""]
      [item """**Bridges**: Arrow C Data Interface (zero-copy in-process
              handoff) and Parquet (cross-ecosystem storage). See
              [[arrow-bridge]] and [[parquet-bridge]]."""]
      [item """**Streaming**: the CX streaming evaluator (see
              [`spec/streaming.md`](../../spec/streaming.md)) emits
              `StartTable` / `RowGroup` / `EndTable` events over CXCol
              data, so directives like `[?for]` and `[?modify]` can
              operate on tables that don't fit in memory."""]
      [item """**Identity**: CXCol files have a stable SHA-256 over their
              canonical (uncompressed) bytes. Compression level does not
              change the hash. Chunk boundaries do — see
              [[cxcol-identity]] for the rule."""]]

    [body
      """The rest of this section walks each piece. The normative spec
         documents are [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10-3.13 (wire format) and
         [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/misc/table-api.md)
         (host binding API). This guide is the working developer
         summary."""]

    [note
      """**Naming.** `CXCol` is the column-oriented streaming format
         name. The `CXDB` / `CXDatabase` namespace is reserved for a
         future query-engine product and does not refer to the file
         format. Throughout this guide,
         CXCol is the format; `.cxcol` is the file extension; the
         `cxcol_*` prefix appears on host-binding identifiers."""]]

  [child id=table-shape n=5.2 title='The :table data shape'
    [intro
      """The `:table` type tag declares a typed columnar table at the
         logical CX layer. It is a CX element like any other — same
         brackets, same parse rules, same canonical-bytes contract — but
         the parser recognizes the `:table` body shape and produces a
         `Table` value rather than a generic element."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[orders :table
           :columns (id :int, customer :string, amount :float, ts :datetime)
           (1, 'Ada',   42.50,  2026-05-22T10:00:00Z)
           (2, 'Grace', 17.25,  2026-05-22T10:15:00Z)
           (3, 'Linus', 99.00,  2026-05-22T11:30:00Z)]"""]

    [body
      """The `:columns` attribute carries the column spec: a parenthesized
         sequence of `name :type` pairs. The body is a sequence of row
         tuples, each tuple matching the column count. Tuple values must
         match the declared column types (or be `null` for nullable
         columns); cell-type mismatches are a parse error
         (`E_TABLE_CELL_TYPE`)."""]

    [body
      """Supported column types match the CX scalar type system:
         `:int` / `:i8..:i64` / `:u8..:u64` (sized integers), `:float` /
         `:f32` / `:f64` / `:f16` (floats), `:bool`, `:string`,
         `:bytes`, `:date`, `:datetime`, `:decimal(precision, scale)`,
         `:bigint`, plus collection columns (`arr[T]`, `map[K, V]`,
         `seq[T]`). The full type catalogue with byte
         encodings is in [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10 + [`spec/misc/type-mapping.md`](../../spec/misc/type-mapping.md)."""]

    [child id=nullable n=5.2.1 title='Nullable columns'
      [body
        """Nullable columns are declared by wrapping the type in
           `nullable`. A null cell is written as the literal `null`."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[customers :table
             :columns (id :int,
                       name :string,
                       phone (nullable :string))
             (1, 'Ada',   '555-1212')
             (2, 'Grace', null)
             (3, 'Linus', '555-9999')]"""]
      [body
        """Nullable wire encoding is the def/rep-level pattern from
           [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md) §3.10.5; the host
           binding's `Table.cell(r, c)` returns the host's null sentinel
           (`None` in Python, `nil` in Go, `Option::None` in Rust)."""]]

    [child id=dict-encoding n=5.2.2 title='Dictionary encoding'
      [body
        """Tables are dictionary-encoded by default for `:string`
           columns with low cardinality. The encoder builds a per-chunk
           dictionary of distinct values; the column buffer stores
           dictionary indices instead of raw strings. Decoders rebuild
           the strings transparently; the host-binding API never
           exposes the indices."""]
      [body
        """Dictionary encoding is automatic, not a user-visible flag.
           The threshold (≥ 4× compression vs. raw on the chunk) lives
           in [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md) §3.10.4. CXPath
           `=` and `[?match]` value comparison operate on the decoded
           string, so user code is dictionary-agnostic."""]]

    [child id=table-validate n=5.2.3 title='Schema validation'
      [body
        """Tables validate against `.cxs` schemas the same way ordinary
           elements do. A schema describes the column spec; the validator
           enforces row-count, type-conformance, and nullability per
           cell. See [[data#schema]] for the schema language and
           [`spec/schema.md`](../../spec/schema.md) §6 for table-specific
           rules."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# orders.cxs — schema describing the orders table shape #]
           [schema
             [table :name orders
               [column :name id       :type int    :required true]
               [column :name customer :type string :required true]
               [column :name amount   :type float  :required true]
               [column :name ts       :type datetime]]]"""]]

    [child id=table-api n=5.2.4 title='The Table host-binding API'
      [intro
        """Bindings expose a `Table` value type when `loads` encounters a
           `:table` block. Methods match across bindings up to host-idiom
           differences; the canonical surface is documented in
           [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/misc/table-api.md)."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           doc = cxlib.loads(open('orders.cx').read())
           t   = doc['orders']                     # Table instance

           print(t.cols)         # ['id', 'customer', 'amount', 'ts']
           print(t.row_count)    # 3
           print(t.row(0))       # OrderedDict([('id', 1), ('customer', 'Ada'), ...])
           print(t.column('amount'))  # [42.50, 17.25, 99.00]

           # Slicing / projection
           sub = t.head(2).select(['customer', 'amount'])
           print(sub.to_cx())"""]
      [body
        """The 17-member canonical surface (`cols` / `types` / `row` /
           `column` / `cell` / `slice` / `head` / `tail` / `select` /
           iteration + adapters) is described in
           [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/misc/table-api.md) §3. The same
           shape exists in V, Go, Rust, and TypeScript with the naming
           conventions of each host."""]]]

  [child id=cxcol n=5.3 title='CXCol — column-oriented streaming format'
    [intro
      """CXCol is the physical wire form for tabular data: column-oriented,
         row-group-chunked, content-addressable, and streamable in bounded
         memory. The data-bin tag is `0x63`; the file extension is
         `.cxcol`. The normative byte-level layout is in
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md) §3.11."""]

    [body
      """The format is a single sequential pass: header, schema preamble,
         then a stream of row groups, then a terminator. Writers emit one
         row group at a time and can produce a billion-row table in
         memory bounded by the largest row group. Readers pull row groups
         one at a time and can skip groups by their length prefix without
         decoding them."""]

    [child id=cxcol-layout n=5.3.1 title='Wire layout'
      [body
        """A CXCol stream is:"""]
      [list
        [item """**12-byte header.** Magic `CXDB`, format version,
                capability bits, header flags. Header flag bit 0 reserved;
                header flag bit 1 signals schema-driven mode (see
                [[cxcol-schema-driven]])."""]
        [item """**Schema preamble.** The column spec: count, then one
                `(name, type_tag, nullable_flag)` triple per column.
                Encoded once at the head — every row group in the file
                conforms to this schema."""]
        [item """**Row-group sequence.** Each group is
                `uvarint(body_byte_len) <body-tag> <body>`. `body-tag`
                `0x01` = plain (uncompressed column buffers); `0x90` =
                compressed with codec id (`0x01` = zstd v1)."""]
        [item """**Per-chunk manifest** (inside each group). Row count,
                optional per-column min/max/null-count statistics. The
                statistics are not yet used by libcx itself but are
                reserved space the Parquet bridge populates on emit."""]
        [item """**Terminator.** A single `0x00` byte (zero-length
                row group) closes the stream."""]]
      [body
        """The 2²⁰ (1,048,576) rows per group is the canonical chunk
           size — `cx canon` enforces it on a CXCol input, and writers
           targeting cross-system hash invariance should use it.
           Different chunk sizes produce valid CXCol files but with
           different SHA-256 hashes."""]]

    [child id=cxcol-identity n=5.3.2 title='Content-addressable identity'
      [body
        """A CXCol file's SHA-256 hash is computed over its **canonical
           uncompressed bytes** — the bytes you would see if every
           `0x90` page-compression wrapper were unwrapped to its `0x01`
           plain form. Compression is a transport / storage choice;
           identity is a content choice; the format keeps them
           orthogonal."""]
      [list
        [item """**Same data + same chunking + same schema → same hash**,
                regardless of zstd level or whether compression was
                applied at all."""]
        [item """**Different chunk size → different hash.** Chunk
                boundaries are part of the canonical form. The
                2²⁰-rows-per-group convention is the cross-writer
                interoperability rule."""]
        [item """**Hash composes with [[hash]].** The same SHA-256
                primitive that hashes a CX document hashes a CXCol file —
                tooling pipelines (caching, dedup, signing,
                content-addressable storage) treat both uniformly."""]]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           # Two CXCol writers, same source data, different compression
           cxlib.write_cxcol('a.cxcol', table, compress='zstd:3')
           cxlib.write_cxcol('b.cxcol', table, compress='zstd:9')

           # Files differ on disk:
           open('a.cxcol', 'rb').read() == open('b.cxcol', 'rb').read()  # False

           # But canonical hashes are identical:
           cxlib.cxcol_hash('a.cxcol') == cxlib.cxcol_hash('b.cxcol')    # True"""]]

    [child id=cxcol-streaming-api n=5.3.3 title='Streaming reader / writer'
      [intro
        """Bindings expose a handle-based streaming API mirroring the
           C ABI in
           [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md) §3.11.6
           (10 symbols under capability bit 21). The Python / Go / Rust /
           V shapes follow each host's resource-management idiom."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib

           # Reader — pulls one row group at a time
           with cxlib.cxcol_reader('big.cxcol') as r:
               print(r.schema)            # column spec
               for group in r:            # iterator of row-groups
                   process(group)         # group is a Table

           # Writer — pushes one row group at a time
           with cxlib.cxcol_writer('out.cxcol', schema=cols) as w:
               for chunk in source_of_chunks():
                   w.emit(chunk)          # bounded memory per chunk"""]
      [example lang=go
        """package main

           import "cxlib"

           func main() {
               r, _ := cxlib.CxcolReaderOpen("big.cxcol")
               defer r.Close()
               for {
                   g, err := r.Next()
                   if err == cxlib.EOF { break }
                   process(g)
               }
           }"""]
      [example lang=rust
        """use cxlib::cxcol::Reader;

           let mut r = Reader::open("big.cxcol")?;
           while let Some(group) = r.next()? {
               process(group);
           }
           // r dropped → reader closed via Drop"""]
      [body
        """Memory budget is one row group in flight per stage in the
           pipeline. A reader chained into a CXPath filter chained into a
           Parquet writer holds three row groups at once, total —
           regardless of the table's logical row count. See
           [[streaming]] for the directive-level view."""]]

    [child id=cxcol-schema-driven n=5.3.4 title='Schema-driven mode'
      [body
        """When the writer knows both sides of the wire have access to
           the schema, header flag bit 1 enables schema-driven mode: per-
           value type tags are omitted for columns the schema declares;
           reader and writer walk the schema in lockstep with the data.
           Wire density approaches Parquet's typed encoding."""]
      [body
        """Schema-driven mode is opt-in (`cxlib.write_cxcol(...,
           schema_driven=True)`); having a schema in scope does not
           silently promote the encoder. The schema reference forms
           (`0x10` content-hash, `0x11` inline, `0x12` content-hash +
           name hint) are documented in
           [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md) §3.13."""]
      [note
        """Schema-driven mode requires the reader to retrieve the schema
           by its content hash. Local lookup is the default; the
           callback-shaped content-store ABI is a post-v0.8.0
           follow-up."""]]]

  [child id=arrow-bridge n=5.4 title='Arrow bridge — zero-copy interop'
    [intro
      """CXCol bridges to Apache Arrow via the Arrow **C Data Interface**
         — a struct-based ABI that lets two libraries share columnar
         buffers without copying them. The CX side is `libcx_arrow`, a
         separate dynamic library that depends on libcx + the Arrow
         C-Data ABI. Core libcx is Arrow-free; capability bit 23
         advertises Arrow when libcx_arrow is loaded."""]

    [body
      """The bridge exports four C ABI symbols at v0.8.0:
         `cx_arrow_export_open` / `cx_arrow_export_open_fd` for
         CXCol → Arrow, and `cx_arrow_import_to_data_bin` /
         `cx_arrow_import_to_data_bin_fd` for Arrow → CXCol. Bindings
         wrap them as `to_arrow()` / `from_arrow()` on the Table value
         type."""]

    [child id=arrow-zero-copy n=5.4.1 title='Zero-copy handoff'
      [body
        """The Arrow C Data Interface uses two structs: `ArrowSchema`
           (column types + names) and `ArrowArray` (per-batch buffers,
           including null bitmap, offsets, and values). CX populates
           these structs to point at its own column buffers; consumers
           (DuckDB, Polars, PyArrow, etc.) read from the same memory
           pages CX wrote."""]
      [body
        """Ownership crosses the boundary explicitly. The exporter
           populates an `ArrowArrayStream` with a `release` callback;
           the consumer calls `release` when done. CX's release callback
           frees the underlying CXCol buffers. There is no GC interplay
           — bindings hold the Arrow stream as an opaque handle until
           they're done with it."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib, pyarrow as pa

           # Load CXCol — buffers held by libcx_arrow
           t = cxlib.read_cxcol('orders.cxcol')

           # Zero-copy export to Arrow: no buffer copy, just pointer handoff
           reader = t.to_arrow_stream()          # ArrowArrayStream
           pa_table = pa.Table.from_batches(reader)

           # pa_table now references the same memory as t.
           # Don't free t (or call cxlib release) until pa_table is done."""]
      [example lang=rust
        """use cxlib::cxcol;
           use arrow::record_batch::RecordBatchReader;

           let t = cxcol::read("orders.cxcol")?;
           let mut reader: Box<dyn RecordBatchReader> = t.into_arrow_stream()?;
           while let Some(batch) = reader.next() {
               let batch = batch?;
               // batch.column(0) points at the same memory as t's first column
               process(batch);
           }"""]]

    [child id=arrow-type-mapping n=5.4.2 title='Type mapping'
      [body
        """The CX ↔ Arrow type map is normative at v0.8.0. The
           high-level summary:"""]
      [list
        [item """`:int` / `:i64` ↔ `Int64`; `:i32` ↔ `Int32`; sized
                variants (`:i8..:u64`) map to corresponding Arrow
                sized integer types."""]
        [item """`:float` / `:f64` ↔ `Float64`; `:f32` ↔ `Float32`;
                `:f16` ↔ `Float16` (Arrow ≥ 1.0)."""]
        [item """`:bool` ↔ `Boolean` (bit-packed in Arrow)."""]
        [item """`:string` ↔ `Utf8` (or `LargeUtf8` for columns whose
                total byte length exceeds 2 GB)."""]
        [item """`:bytes` ↔ `Binary` (or `LargeBinary` similarly)."""]
        [item """`:date` ↔ `Date32` (days since 1970-01-01,
                proleptic Gregorian)."""]
        [item """`:datetime` ↔ `Timestamp(Nanoseconds, UTC)`; offsets
                round-trip via the `tz` field when present."""]
        [item """`:decimal(p, s)` ↔ `Decimal128` (p ≤ 38) or `Decimal256`
                (p ≤ 76)."""]
        [item """`arr[T]` ↔ `List<T>`; `map[K, V]` ↔ `Map<K, V>`;
                `seq[T]` ↔ `List<T>` (sequence flatten per CXDM §1.2)."""]
        [item """`nullable T` → Arrow's standard nullable variant
                (validity bitmap)."""]]
      [body
        """v0.8.0 column-type coverage in `libcx_arrow` is 9 of
           10 types (int / i8 / i16 / i32 / float / bool / string / date /
           bytes). `:datetime`, `:decimal`, and dictionary / extension
           columns remain deferred to a future libcx_arrow expansion;
           the wire surface accepts them but the C-Data bridge errors
           with `arrow-014-unsupported-type-deferred-error`."""]]

    [child id=arrow-ipc n=5.4.3 title='Arrow IPC stream format'
      [body
        """Beyond zero-copy in-process handoff, CXCol can emit Arrow IPC
           streams for cross-process or cross-host transport. The IPC
           stream is Arrow's own serialization (schema header + record
           batches + EOF), distinct from the C Data Interface
           (in-memory structs)."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib, pyarrow as pa

           # Emit Arrow IPC stream — bytes you can write to a file or socket
           with open('orders.arrow', 'wb') as f:
               for batch in cxlib.read_cxcol('orders.cxcol').to_arrow_ipc():
                   f.write(batch)"""]
      [body
        """The `cx table dump --to=arrow` CLI subcommand
           (`spec/cx_table_cli.md`) writes the IPC stream form by
           default; `--arrow-format=file` opts into the file-format
           variant."""]]

    [child id=arrow-flight n=5.4.4 title='Arrow Flight (post-v0.8.0)'
      [body
        """Arrow Flight is Arrow's gRPC-based RPC framing for cross-
           process record batches. It is the natural next layer above
           the IPC stream — a Flight server publishes batches by
           descriptor; a Flight client subscribes. CX integration is
           **post-v0.8.0**: no Flight surface ships in v0.8.0; the
           tooling-layer story will be specified when it lands."""]
      [note
        """For cross-process CXCol transport in v0.8.0, use Arrow IPC
           over your existing transport (HTTP, pipe, socket). The
           `[?http-client]` directive can carry Arrow IPC streams as
           binary bodies."""]]]

  [child id=parquet-bridge n=5.5 title='Parquet bridge'
    [intro
      """CXCol bridges to Apache Parquet via Arrow: CXCol → Arrow stream
         → Parquet file. Core libcx has **no direct Parquet C++
         dependency** — adding one would couple every binding to a
         heavyweight build-time toolchain. The Parquet adapter is a
         binding-specific library (PyArrow for Python, arrow-rs for
         Rust, parquet-go for Go, etc.) chained off the Arrow
         bridge."""]

    [child id=parquet-write n=5.5.1 title='Writing Parquet'
      [intro
        """`Table.write_parquet(path, ...)` walks CXCol → Arrow record
           batches → Parquet writer. The Parquet writer is the host
           binding's choice; the CXCol side just provides the Arrow
           stream."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib

           t = cxlib.read_cxcol('orders.cxcol')
           t.write_parquet(
               'orders.parquet',
               compression='snappy',     # Parquet ecosystem default
               row_group_size=1_048_576, # match CXCol canonical chunk
               use_dictionary=True,      # dictionary encoding (per-col)
               write_statistics=True,    # min/max/null per row-group
           )"""]
      [body
        """Defaults are the Parquet-ecosystem defaults (Snappy, not
           zstd) — output is meant to be consumed by Parquet readers
           that assume Snappy. Zstd is CX's default for CXCol's `0x90`
           page compression; the two formats use their own ecosystem
           defaults to minimize friction with the consumers of
           each."""]
      [example lang=rust
        """use cxlib::cxcol;
           use parquet::file::writer::*;
           use parquet::basic::Compression;

           let t = cxcol::read("orders.cxcol")?;
           let mut writer = SerializedFileWriter::new(
               std::fs::File::create("orders.parquet")?,
               t.arrow_schema()?,
               Compression::SNAPPY.into(),
           )?;
           for batch in t.into_arrow_stream()? {
               writer.write(&batch?)?;
           }
           writer.close()?;"""]]

    [child id=parquet-read n=5.5.2 title='Reading Parquet'
      [intro
        """`read_parquet(path, ...)` is the symmetric inverse. The
           Parquet reader produces Arrow record batches; CX wraps them
           as a streaming Table."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib

           # Whole-file read
           t = cxlib.read_parquet('orders.parquet')
           print(t.row_count)

           # Streaming read with projection and predicate
           for chunk in cxlib.read_parquet_chunks(
                   'orders.parquet',
                   columns=['id', 'amount'],     # only read these columns
                   filter='amount > 100'):        # predicate pushdown via stats
               process(chunk)"""]
      [body
        """Predicate pushdown uses per-row-group min/max statistics
           emitted by the Parquet writer. A predicate like `amount > 100`
           against a row group whose `max(amount) = 50` skips the
           group's data pages entirely. Column projection only reads the
           requested column chunks. Together, selective queries on wide
           tables can reduce I/O by 10× or more — see
           [[performance]]."""]
      [note
        """Predicate pushdown depends on the Parquet writer having
           emitted statistics. The CX writer (5.5.1) does so by default
           (`write_statistics=True`). Files produced by other writers
           may not have statistics — the reader still works, but
           degrades to a full scan."""]]]

    [child id=parquet-types n=5.5.3 title='CX ↔ Parquet type mapping'
      [body
        """The mapping is normative at v0.8.0. Most types round-trip
           cleanly through Arrow; a small
           number of CX types have lossy edges that `--strict` mode
           rejects rather than silently coerces."""]
      [list
        [item """`:int` / `:i64` → `INT64`."""]
        [item """`:i32` → `INT32`; `:i16` / `:i8` → `INT32` with
                `INT(N, true)` logical type; unsigned similarly."""]
        [item """`:f64` / `:float` → `DOUBLE`; `:f32` → `FLOAT`;
                `:f16` → `FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY(2)` / `FLOAT16`
                (the latter is non-standard pre-Parquet 1.13)."""]
        [item """`:bool` → `BOOLEAN`."""]
        [item """`:string` → `BYTE_ARRAY` with `STRING` logical type
                (UTF-8)."""]
        [item """`:bytes` → `BYTE_ARRAY`."""]
        [item """`:date` → `INT32` with `DATE` logical type."""]
        [item """`:datetime` (UTC) → `INT64` with
                `TIMESTAMP(NANOS, isAdjustedToUTC=true)`."""]
        [item """`:decimal(p, s)` → `DECIMAL(p, s)` (Parquet caps at
                256-bit precision)."""]
        [item """`:bigint` → `Decimal256(38, 0)` with diagnostic;
                `--strict` rejects."""]
        [item """`arr[T]` → `LIST<T-projection>`."""]
        [item """`map[K, V]` → `MAP<K, V>` (string-keyed at v0.8.0)."""]
        [item """`seq[T]` → `LIST<T-projection>` after sequence flatten;
                `--strict` rejects (re-import produces `arr[T]`, not
                `seq[T]`)."""]
        [item """`nullable T` → `OPTIONAL` repetition."""]]]

    [child id=parquet-roundtrip n=5.5.4 title='Round-trip identity'
      [body
        """CX → Parquet → CX is not in general byte-identical. Parquet
           is a presentation-layer format: footer offsets shift,
           row-group sizes are writer-dependent, encoding choices
           (dictionary thresholds, page sizes, compression codec) are
           per-writer. Many byte-level Parquet files encode the same
           logical data. CXCol fills this niche precisely — it is the
           hashable origin; Parquet is the bridge."""]
      [body
        """**Logical** round-trip is normative: a CX table emitted as
           Parquet and re-imported via `cx table load` produces the
           same `:table` block with the same column names, types, row
           count, and cell values (modulo the documented lossy edges in
           5.5.3). The `cx_eq` primitive operates on the logical level
           and reports equality after round-trip."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           t1 = cxlib.loads(open('orders.cx').read())['orders']
           t1.write_parquet('orders.parquet')
           t2 = cxlib.read_parquet('orders.parquet')
           assert t1 == t2     # logical equality holds
           assert t1.to_cxcol_bytes() == t2.to_cxcol_bytes()
                               # canonical CXCol bytes match too"""]]

    [child id=parquet-metadata n=5.5.5 title='Metadata preservation'
      [body
        """Parquet's `KeyValueMetadata` is a list of UTF-8 key-value
           pairs at the file footer. CXCol metadata slots — column
           comments, table-level annotations, the original schema
           content-hash — map to entries with the `cx_*` prefix
           (`cx_schema_hash`, `cx_schema_inline`, `cx_column_doc.<name>`).
           Readers that don't recognize the prefix ignore it; CX
           readers re-attach the metadata on import."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib

           t = cxlib.read_cxcol('orders.cxcol')
           # Strict-schema mode embeds the CXCol schema as Parquet KV metadata
           t.write_parquet('orders.parquet', strict_schema=True)

           # Re-import preserves schema identity
           t2 = cxlib.read_parquet('orders.parquet')
           assert t.schema_hash() == t2.schema_hash()"""]
      [note
        """Default writer mode does NOT embed the schema hash — Parquet
           consumers in other ecosystems wouldn't know what to do with
           it, and adding unrecognized metadata is a friction point.
           Use `--strict-schema` (CLI) / `strict_schema=True` (binding)
           when round-trip integrity matters."""]]]

  [child id=streaming n=5.6 title='Streaming evaluator over columnar data'
    [intro
      """The CX streaming evaluator
         ([`spec/streaming.md`](../../spec/streaming.md)) operates over
         CXCol chunks one at a time. The gate-15 perf target is **≥ 200
         MB/s sustained on JSON-shape workloads** (per
         [`spec/v0_8_0_status.md`](../../spec/v0_8_0_status.md) gate 15);
         the v0.8.0 measurement runs green at 353 MB/s on the
         realistic-marshaling bench."""]

    [body
      """The streaming evaluator emits three table-specific events when
         it encounters a CXCol input: **StartTable** (column spec),
         **RowGroup** (one chunk's data), **EndTable** (close).
         Directives that consume the stream see one row group at a time
         and never materialize the full table."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[# Filter a 100GB CXCol file — never loads it whole.
         The [?for] consumes one row group at a time; [?yield] emits
         a new CXCol stream downstream. Memory is bounded by the
         largest row group, not the table size. #]
         [?for [in $row [?cx-read 'big.cxcol']]
           [where [> $row/@amount 1000]]
           [yield [transaction id=$row/@id amount=$row/@amount]]]"""]

    [body
      """The directive-level view: any directive that takes a CXPath
         iterator works on a CXCol stream. `[?for]` / `[?modify]` /
         `[?map]` / `[?reduce]` (see [[code]]) all participate.
         Bounded memory is preserved end-to-end: a pipeline of N stages
         holds N row groups in flight, not N × row-count."""]

    [child id=streaming-events n=5.6.1 title='Streaming events'
      [body
        """The event vocabulary
           ([`spec/streaming.md`](../../spec/streaming.md) §1) extends
           the standard `StartElement` / `Scalar` / `EndElement` events
           with three table-specific variants."""]
      [list
        [item """**StartTable** — fields: `name` (string),
                `col_spec` (binary blob; the column schema in ast_bin
                form). Emitted in place of the `StartElement` that
                would otherwise wrap a `:table` body."""]
        [item """**RowGroup** — fields: `row_count` (u32), `payload`
                (binary blob; one row group's column buffers). One
                event per row group, in source order."""]
        [item """**EndTable** — fields: `name` (string). Closes the
                sequence in place of the corresponding `EndElement`."""]]
      [body
        """A non-chunked `0x60` (whole-table) input emits the existing
           `StartElement` / per-cell-`Scalar` / `EndElement` sequence
           unchanged. Only `0x63` chunked-CXCol inputs produce the new
           events. Streaming consumers that don't care about the
           distinction can flatten via a helper that re-emits each
           `RowGroup` as a sequence of `StartElement` / row /
           `EndElement` triples."""]]

    [child id=streaming-perf n=5.6.2 title='Performance posture'
      [body
        """The streaming evaluator's optimization stack at v0.8.0
           includes pre-compiled CXPath, env-rooted memoization,
           pointer-arithmetic emit into a pre-grown buffer, and a 64 KiB
           flush threshold tuned for page-cluster alignment. The full
           inventory and the path to higher throughput
           (`-prealloc` default, parse-once API, JIT) is recorded in
           the streaming-perf session memory."""]
      [note
        """The 200 MB/s target is **realistic-workload** (JSON-shape
           records, ~75 B/record). The toy 25-byte `id:name;` bench
           hits a lower number because it's loop-control-bound, not
           marshaling-bound. Cite the realistic figure externally."""]]]

  [child id=integrations n=5.7 title='Ecosystem integrations'
    [intro
      """The Arrow C Data Interface is the integration layer the
         analytical ecosystem standardized on. Once CX exposes Arrow,
         the rest of the stack lights up — DuckDB, Polars, DataFusion,
         pandas, Spark via Parquet — most with zero-copy handoff in
         process, the rest with one copy at the boundary."""]

    [child id=duckdb n=5.7.1 title='DuckDB'
      [body
        """DuckDB consumes Arrow via its `arrow_scan` registration
           function — zero-copy from CXCol's column buffers into
           DuckDB's vectorized executor. The integration ships as a
           binding-level idiom (`cxlib.idioms.to_duckdb()`)."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib, duckdb

           t = cxlib.read_cxcol('orders.cxcol')
           con = duckdb.connect()

           # Register the table — zero-copy via Arrow C Data Interface
           con.register('orders', t.to_arrow())

           # Now query with SQL
           result = con.execute('''
               SELECT customer, SUM(amount) AS total
               FROM orders
               GROUP BY customer
               ORDER BY total DESC
           ''').fetchall()"""]
      [body
        """DuckDB's executor reads directly from CX's column buffers
           during the scan. The result set is materialized into
           DuckDB's own memory; converting back to CXCol with
           `cxlib.cxcol_from_duckdb(con, 'orders')` is the symmetric
           inverse."""]]

    [child id=polars n=5.7.2 title='Polars'
      [body
        """Polars is Arrow-native at the buffer level. CX → Polars is a
           PyArrow round-trip in Python, or a direct
           `polars::DataFrame::from_arrow` in Rust — zero-copy in both
           cases for the underlying buffers."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib, polars as pl, pyarrow as pa

           t = cxlib.read_cxcol('orders.cxcol')
           df = pl.from_arrow(pa.Table.from_batches(t.to_arrow_stream()))

           # Polars query
           result = df.lazy().filter(pl.col('amount') > 100).collect()"""]
      [example lang=rust
        """use cxlib::cxcol;
           use polars::prelude::*;

           let t = cxcol::read("orders.cxcol")?;
           let df = DataFrame::from_arrow(t.into_arrow_stream()?)?;
           let result = df.lazy()
               .filter(col("amount").gt(100))
               .collect()?;"""]]

    [child id=pandas n=5.7.3 title='pandas'
      [body
        """pandas is **not** zero-copy at the buffer level — its column
           model uses NumPy arrays with type-system mismatches against
           Arrow (no nullable integers, no first-class strings without
           StringDtype). CX → pandas is one Arrow round-trip plus one
           pandas conversion; expect one full data copy at the
           boundary."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib

           t = cxlib.read_cxcol('orders.cxcol')

           # Convenience adapter; one copy under the hood
           df = t.to_pandas()

           # Equivalent two-step (PyArrow → pandas)
           import pyarrow as pa
           df = pa.Table.from_batches(t.to_arrow_stream()).to_pandas()"""]
      [note
        """For DataFrame-style work where zero-copy matters, prefer
           Polars over pandas. pandas remains supported because the
           Python data-science ecosystem assumes it, but the
           cross-boundary cost is real on large tables."""]]

    [child id=datafusion n=5.7.4 title='DataFusion (post-v0.8.0)'
      [body
        """Apache DataFusion is a Rust query engine over Arrow. Its
           `TableProvider` trait accepts arbitrary Arrow record-batch
           sources, so CXCol is a natural fit: register a `.cxcol` file
           as a DataFusion table, run SQL or DataFrame queries against
           it with the same zero-copy guarantees as Polars."""]
      [body
        """Status: **post-v0.8.0**. A `libcx_query` C ABI is
           **drafted** but not in scope for v0.8.0. Adopters who need DataFusion
           today wire it manually via the Arrow bridge — the path
           works, it just lacks the convenience layer."""]
      [example lang=rust
        """// Manual wiring at v0.8.0 — convenience layer post-v0.8.0
           use cxlib::cxcol;
           use datafusion::prelude::*;

           let ctx = SessionContext::new();
           let batches: Vec<_> = cxcol::read("orders.cxcol")?
               .into_arrow_stream()?
               .collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;
           let mem_table = MemTable::try_new(batches[0].schema(), vec![batches])?;
           ctx.register_table("orders", Arc::new(mem_table))?;

           let df = ctx.sql("SELECT customer, SUM(amount) FROM orders GROUP BY customer").await?;
           df.show().await?;"""]]

    [child id=spark n=5.7.5 title='Spark and cluster compute'
      [body
        """Spark reads / writes Parquet natively. CXCol's Parquet bridge
           (see [[parquet-bridge]]) is the integration path — emit
           `.parquet` from CX, point Spark at the directory, run
           PySpark / Spark SQL. For zero-copy in cluster contexts,
           Arrow Flight is the long-term path; status is
           **post-v0.8.0** per [[arrow-flight]]."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           from pyspark.sql import SparkSession

           # CXCol → Parquet (one-time conversion)
           cxlib.read_cxcol('orders.cxcol').write_parquet('orders.parquet')

           # Spark reads the Parquet directly
           spark = SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()
           df = spark.read.parquet('orders.parquet')
           df.groupBy('customer').sum('amount').show()"""]
      [body
        """The Parquet bridge is one-way at this layer — Spark's writes
           come back through CXCol via `cx table load
           output.parquet --to=cx`. For round-trip workflows, keep the
           CXCol file as the canonical origin and treat Spark's outputs
           as derived artifacts."""]]

  [child id=performance n=5.8 title='Performance characteristics'
    [intro
      """The analytics lane's perf budgets and where they're measured.
         Numbers below are the spec gates (target floor) and the most
         recent measurement on the v0.8.0 branch. Re-run
         instructions live in the per-bench `Makefile` targets."""]

    [list
      [item """**Streaming throughput (JSON-shape, ~75 B/record).**
              Spec gate: mean ≥ 200 MB/s, no trial < 80% of mean.
              Measured at v0.8.0 at 353 MB/s. Source:
              [`spec/v0_8_0_status.md`](../../spec/v0_8_0_status.md)
              gate 15; bench: `bench-code-streaming`."""]
      [item """**Parquet write (default Snappy, 2²⁰ row-group).**
              Measured via PyArrow's writer on the CXCol → Arrow → Parquet
              path; throughput is dominated by the Parquet codec, not
              the CXCol bridge. Adopters should profile against their
              chosen Parquet writer; no normative CX-side gate."""]
      [item """**Arrow zero-copy handoff.** O(metadata) per batch —
              the schema and per-column buffer pointers are populated,
              no buffer is copied. Time per `to_arrow()` is independent
              of row count and proportional to column count."""]
      [item """**Predicate pushdown reduction.** Selective queries
              (predicate matches < 10% of row groups) typically reduce
              I/O by 10× or more vs. full scan, when the Parquet writer
              emitted statistics. Below 1% selectivity, Bloom filters
              (Parquet-side, out of scope here) become relevant."""]
      [item """**Memory budget per stream stage.** One row group in
              flight. The streaming evaluator
              ([`spec/streaming.md`](../../spec/streaming.md) §4) is
              bounded by `max(row_group_byte_size)` regardless of
              table size."""]]

    [note
      """The 200 MB/s gate is for the **streaming evaluator**, not the
         raw CXCol reader. The reader is faster (no evaluator pipeline);
         the writer is comparable. Per-binding adapter throughput
         (`to_pandas()` etc.) varies by host — pandas is the slowest
         because of the boundary copy."""]]

  [child id=cxcol-vs-others n=5.9 title='When to use CXCol vs Parquet vs Arrow vs JSON'
    [intro
      """Pick by use case. The four formats overlap in the analytics
         lane but each has a niche the others fill badly."""]

    [list
      [item """**CXCol** — use when CX content-addressable identity
              matters (caching, dedup, signing, provenance) or when
              you want streaming with the CX type system end to end.
              Single-writer, sequential-decode, hashable. Single-file
              format; not designed for query (use the Arrow bridge into
              DuckDB / Polars / DataFusion for that)."""]
      [item """**Parquet** — use for cross-ecosystem interop (Spark,
              BigQuery, Snowflake, Trino, Athena, all the Hadoop-
              lineage tools). Lossy on CX-specific metadata by default
              (round-trip preservation requires `--strict-schema`).
              Not hashable in the byte-stable sense — different writers
              produce different bytes for the same logical data."""]
      [item """**Arrow IPC stream** — use for in-process or LAN-local
              zero-copy between CX and another Arrow-native consumer
              (DuckDB, Polars, DataFusion, pandas via PyArrow). No
              compression by default (Arrow is layout-oriented, not
              compression-oriented); not designed for long-term
              storage."""]
      [item """**JSON / JSONL** — use for ad-hoc human-readable interop
              or when the consumer truly is JSON-shaped. Lossy on type
              metadata (no distinction between `:int` and `:float`,
              date-times become strings, dictionary encoding gone). The
              CX → JSON projection (see [[surfaces#json]]) handles the
              shape conversion; for tabular data specifically, JSONL
              (one row per line) is the conventional wire form."""]]

    [body
      """The decision tree, compressed:"""]

    [list
      [item """Need a hashable, content-addressable origin? → **CXCol**."""]
      [item """Sharing with Spark / Snowflake / BigQuery / other
              non-Arrow-native tools? → **Parquet**."""]
      [item """In-process handoff to DuckDB / Polars / DataFusion /
              pandas? → **Arrow** (with CXCol as the origin)."""]
      [item """Debugging, ad-hoc inspection, or a JSON-shaped consumer? →
              **JSON / JSONL**."""]]

    [body
      """In practice the layering is: **CXCol as canonical origin →
         Arrow as the in-process bridge → Parquet for cross-ecosystem
         export**. JSON is the human-readable view. The same logical
         table can move through all four; identity stays at CXCol."""]]

  [child id=see-also n=5.10 title='See also'
    [list
      [item """[[identity]] — canonical bytes + SHA-256. CXCol identity
              rests on the same SHA-256 primitive over canonical bytes;
              the rule extends naturally to chunked tables and
              schema-driven encoding."""]
      [item """[[surfaces]] — the JSON / YAML / TOML / XML / Markdown
              projections. CXCol is the analytical surface; the others
              are the interchange surfaces."""]
      [item """[[bindings]] — Layer 1 / Layer 2 host idioms for reading
              and writing CXCol in V / Python / Go / Rust. The Table
              API and the streaming reader / writer live there."""]
      [item """[[code#streaming]] — the directive-level streaming view:
              `[?for]` / `[?modify]` over CXCol inputs in bounded
              memory."""]
      [item """[`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/misc/table-api.md) — the
              17-member typed columnar Table API ratified across all
              bindings."""]
      [item """[`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md) §3.10-3.13 —
              chunked-table wire format, page-compression wrapper,
              schema-driven mode."""]
      [item """[`spec/streaming.md`](../../spec/streaming.md) — streaming
              evaluator design and the StartTable / RowGroup / EndTable
              event vocabulary."""]]]]

[# End of section 5 — analytics #]
